    "turn_text": "off, I guess. if I can say that. I'll tell you something I probably never shared before as part of the story. So when the Coleman random gets reversed, repealed, overturned, I immediately call Jeff Sessions and need to speak to the Attorney General. And I can remember I was in the basement of the Russell Senate building when his call came in. And I'm kind of looking around in the middle of the hallway saying, okay, I gotta take this call, but I don't know where to go. So I ducked into the Senate barbershop and I'm on the phone and I'm like in some room back there with Jeff Sessions. And I remember this conversation because Jeff said the Attorney General said, well, this really isn't a big deal. It doesn't really, I don't think anybody's going to even notice that we did this. Mr. Attorney General, General Sessions, with all due respect, this is a big deal. It's a big deal to Colorado. It's a big deal to the issue. This is a big deal. Businesses, patients, and the states more and more across the country that are going this direction. At that conversation, it just made me even more angry about what had happened. And I was angry too, because I felt like he had not told me the truth. Yeah, that in the lead up to his confirmation, he had said that there would be no change in policy. He wouldn't change directions. It wouldn't be a focus. And then all of a sudden, without any kind of, you know, heads up about this and maybe it wouldn't have made a difference even if he did give us a heads up. But all of a sudden here, we have this new policy completely goes back on what he told me. And then a conversation where there seems to be a level of sort of a tone deafness that was just shocking on this.",
