  {
    "episode_id": 94305,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/4/94305.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "barbershop to the White House. And so I'm at the White House and it was on trade. The meeting was set up, you know, a week or two earlier on trade issues. and tariffs. And so we went through the meeting, and after the meeting was over, I said, Mr. President, I need to speak to you about something else that happened today. And I said, Jeff Sessions, and he interrupted, and he, oh yeah, he needs to, we need to undo this. He needs to stop this. And it was very clear to me at that point that there was a disagreement between the president and the attorney general on this. And he went a little bit further, said, look, I don't like this. This isn't something I support, but the cats out of the bag, and it's not going back, or the ketchup's out of the bottle. One of those great analogies. Toothpaste out of the toothpaste. Yeah, toothpaste out of the bag. But this was in person, in person, in the White House, in the Oval Office. And he said this, and he goes, we need to just move forward. I think his exact statement was, this sounds like something my grandpa said in the 1950s. And so at that point, I realized that there was an ally in the president on this, and I kept encouraging the press to ask Jeff Sessions. Do you agree or disagree with the president on this? I don't know that anybody did, but I kept trying to push people to it. I kept trying to push people to it and in subsequent conversations with the president, he's never backed away from that commitment. He's always said the same thing and remained consistent and I think understands the need to have this federal fix.",
